<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Quadratic Equations</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
     TIn this section we will study quadratic equations. Any equation where the highest power is <m>x^2</m> is a quadratic equation. 
     For example, <m>y = 3x^2-7x+12</m> is a quadratic equation. Note: <em>quad</em> = square and we read <m>x^2</m> as <q>x-squared</q>. But for quadratic equations, most
     things come in threes. There can be three terms in a quadratic equation. There are three types of solutions for a quadratic equation.
     There are three ways to write a quadratic equation and there are three ways to solve a quadratic equation, which will be the main focus of this section.
    </p>
  </introduction>
